<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">often dated to <a href="/years/1746">1746</a> and linked with the Jacobite rising of <a href="/years/1745">1745</a>; printed by <a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond&quot;, or simply &quot;Loch Lomond&quot;, is a traditional Scottish folk song (Roud 9598), named after the largest loch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>; in Scots &quot;bonnie&quot; means fair or beautiful. Its maker is unknown. It has often been dated to 1746, and its origins are thought to lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a>, with which it has long been associated. Two readings of the words are common.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</b>&quot;, or simply &quot;Loch Lomond&quot;, is a traditional Scottish folk song (Roud 9598), named after the largest loch in Scotland; in Scots &quot;bonnie&quot; means fair or beautiful.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a></li></ul></section>
